#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-modes.rst:5
#: 8867184a48904487ad8b80c2678de80a
msgid "Balance Bot Control Modes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-modes.rst:6
#: a8c159dd9c6c46dc9e76d044e0b9564e
msgid "Some Rover Control Mode work slightly different in Balance Bots. This section explains some of the important differences to be kept in mind while using these modes. For more information on any mode, check the :ref:`Control Modes<rover-control-modes>` page for Rover."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-modes.rst:9
#: f05f0dceeb8f426cbe43becc1d3b96e4
msgid "Hold/Manual"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-modes.rst:10
#: 6f5b1b5a30ff440fad642ee77c71a9d0
msgid "Hold and Manual Mode in Balance Bots use a Pitch Control System to control and maintain pitch angle. In Hold Mode, the input pitch to the control system is set to 0 degrees, to keep the Vehicle upright(equivalent to zero throttle input). In Manual Mode the input throttle from the user is mapped to a pitch angle and sent to the Control System. The control system in turn calculates the required throttle and sends it to the motors. As explained in the :ref:`working <balance_bot-working>` section, this causes the vehicle to accelerate in the direction of pitch. The steering input from the user is handled the same way as in Rover."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-modes.rst:15
#: bea2e21a48a14c989006cd4e5c4aa0e4
msgid "In Manual Mode, continuous acceleration in one direction can saturate the motors, as they reach maximum speed. The vehicle will not be able to balance as further acceleration is not possible. This is the disadvantage of running Balance Bots in Manual Mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-modes.rst:17
#: 0cb8194962c7428eb7e3d98d65f100bf
msgid "In Manual and Hold modes, we can only control the pitch angle of the vehicle, not the velocity. It is possible to be at zero pitch angle(upright position) and have a non zero velocity. To stop the vehicle from drifting in Manual mode, it must be made to pitch in the reverse direction. If these modes are entered with a non zero initial speed(from Acro or Auto modes), the vehicle can accelerate and crash."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-modes.rst:20
#: 6182420788f0485a8b21685cabd86b06
msgid "Acro"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-modes.rst:21
#: 2fdfbdc9eda64b828e7533bee2152f90
msgid "This is the recommended drive mode for Balance Bots."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-modes.rst:23
#: 86253eea3ef34284ac8e4302649cd165
msgid "Acro Mode lets you control the speed by internally controlling the pitch angle. Separately, it also allows you to control the turn rate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-modes.rst:27
#: 510aa4d1dc714ea7844c35d98d2488f0
msgid "Acro mode uses a speed and turn rate control system. In Rover the output from the Acro control system goes straight to the motors. In Balance Bots, the output from the Speed Controller is mapped to a desired pitch and sent to the Pitch Controller used in Manual/Hold Mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-modes.rst:31
#: a9b39bc82cbe4bd7bd9138dbca412b59
msgid "Guided/Auto/RTL"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/balance_bot-modes.rst:32
#: ba907e58d2f34681958585b08142729d
msgid "All of these modes are implemented as they are in Rover. No Balance Bot specific differences exist. Instructions on configuring and using these modes can be found in the :ref:`Rover Control Modes page<rover-control-modes>`."
msgstr ""
